{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc59QhUg2cYVDKaerw44", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7541, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7541}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e3905de0-dd2c-4b7c-8398-0f144123608d", "uuid": "f7688add-1c8f-43b0-859a-a923a7c08c9d", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T19:30:34.920Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfc59QTaYmHJZd2GxtqxZ"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cfc59QhUg2cYVDKaerw44", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Lists and tuples both hold ordered sequences of items. The main difference is that **lists are mutable and tuples are immutable**.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` or `1, 2, 3` |\n| Mutable? | Yes, you can add, remove and change items | No, it's fixed once created |\n| Methods | Many: `append`, `extend`, `insert`, `remove`, `pop`, `sort`, `reverse`\u2026 | Two: `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable? | No | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Memory/speed | Slightly larger and slower | Slightly smaller and faster to create |\n| Typical use | A collection of similar items that changes in size | A fixed group of values whose position gives meaning |\n\n### Mutability in practice\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 10        # fine\nnums.append(4)      # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 10       # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\nA tuple's immutability is **shallow**. If a tuple holds a mutable object, that object can still change:\n```python\nt = ([1, 2], 3)\nt[0].append(99)     # works: t is now ([1, 2, 99], 3)\n```\n\n### Hashability\nBecause tuples can be hashed, you can use them as dictionary keys or put them in sets. Lists can't be used either way:\n```python\nlocations = {(40.7, -74.0): \"NYC\"}   # OK\n{[40.7, -74.0]: \"NYC\"}               # TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n```\n\n### Single-item tuples\nThe comma is what makes a tuple, not the parentheses:\n```python\n(5)    # just the int 5\n(5,)   # a tuple with one item\n```\n\n### Which to use\n- **List:** a group of similar items that you'll add to, remove from or sort, like `users = [...]`.\n- **Tuple:** a fixed record where each position has a meaning, like `(x, y)`, `(name, age)` or several return values (`return a, b`). Use a tuple when you need a hashable key. For records that are easier to read, look at `collections.namedtuple` or `typing.NamedTuple`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7541, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16830, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7541}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e3905de0-dd2c-4b7c-8398-0f144123608d", "uuid": "8c648afb-a7e5-4343-b8e4-c42ab20633e4", "timestamp": "2026-10-01T19:30:41.633Z", "request_id": "req_011Cfc59QTaYmHJZd2GxtqxZ"}
